il fuels like  diesel  gasoline or jet fuel or even create  carbon negative fuels from biological  feedstocks  and moving into the future as hydrogen  goes to displace fossil fuels in a  variety of sectors of our economy  our system can provide scalable ultra  clean  low-cost hydrogen production to fuel the  emerging hydrogen economy  and with that being said i would like to  thank you all very much for listening  and i'll pass it back over to you phil  thank you so much brock  thank you next up we have  sami khan sammy is an assistant  professor at simon fraser university  sammy the floor is yours thank you very  much phil  as phil mentioned i am an assistant  professor in sustainable energy  engineering at simon fraser university  and my research group focuses on  development of materials and coatings  to enhance the performance and longevity  of sustainable energy systems  with a particular focus on hydrogen  energy which i will be presenting  next so as we saw from the panelists  here  there is a lot of advancement in the  development of hydrogen energy  and specifically this has led to the  development of hydrogen economy  which is involving uh which as it's  expanding as you can see from my slide  here on the left  would lead to the development of tanks  which would be need to store which would  be needed to store hydrogen  uh pipelines for the transportation and  systems that can deliver and dispense  hydrogen to end users like ourselves  now as a panelist mentioned a lot of  these systems that are used for fossil  fuels  were constructed using steel but steel  has a major complication when it  interacts with hydrogen which you can  see from the middle panel  that when a steel wire which is  typically very easy to bend and flexible  it can catastrophically fracture when it  is exposed to hydrogen for a long amount  of time  so this dangerous effect is called  hydrogen embrittlement  and it can reduce the performance of  some of these steel components  and very fundamentally it occurs when  hydrogen enters into the steel  and causes it to lose strength and  become brittle so in our research  group we are developing these smart  self-healing coatings to  fundamentally stop this hydrogen from  entering and causing embrittlement  now to explain what i mean by these  smart self-healing coatings we can  show their effectiveness on these  polymers that swell  when they are exposed to water so if you  see on the the panel on the right with  experiment one  when this polymer which is uh very small  is uh inserted into water  it can swell up to many times its own  volume within five minutes  but then in the experiment two when this  is coated with teflon  interestingly even teflon is not able to  block the water from entering  and as you may know teflon is a  well-known water repelling material that  is used to  coat cooking pans but if this teflon has  defects and holes then water can find  its way to go in  and then it can cause this polymer to  swell  but when we use these smart self-healing  coatings which experiment three shows  these coatings are able which are  essentially low surface tension fluids  they're able to cover up these holes and  defects and provide protection  so even after five minutes the polymer  doesn't swell and we show that when we  apply this on steel the hydrogen  diffusivity can be  reduced by 100 times and therefore  provide protection to these  steel components so i'd like to conclude  by saying that  hydrogen energy is very promising to all  of us  and we can envision using this as a  clean and sustainable  fuel to drive our cars or heat our homes  but it is very important that this  hydrogen be delivered both safely and  reliably  so it is important to be aware of  hydrogen embrittlement and have  technologies like these self-healing  coatings which can stop it from  occurring  and this can lead to widespread  protection in many hydrogen technologies  and also as the panelists mentioned with  impacting codes and standards and  reducing costs  towards driving this hydrogen economy uh  thank you very much for this opportunity  and thanks for your attention  thank you sami that was wonderful next  up  we have shu liu phd student at cornell  university  shou please go ahead  okay thank you so much phil and  hi everyone it is such an honor to be  here  i'm studio i'm a phd student from  cornell university  my research is about solar water  splitting  so i'd like to take this opportunity to  give you a brief introduction about my  research field  okay now let's get started  everyone said that hydrogen is the next  big thing  as a green energy resource but is  hatching really green  today 95 of hydrogen produced in the  united states  is by a process called natural gas  reforming  it is energy intensive and it makes  carbon dioxide  as a by-product therefore we need to  find  alternative methods to produce hydrogen  in a greener way  studio i'm a phd student in mechanical  and aerospace  engineering at cornell university i have  been conducting research  on efficient hatred production by solar  water splitting which could change how  hydrogen is being produced  think of the process like this you shine  sunlight on the system  to convert water into hydrogen and  oxygen  imagine being able to use sun to get  fuel from water  which is everywhere on the planet this  could potentially revolutionize  hydrogen production and make hydrogen  a fully renewable energy resource now  let's look at the factors affecting the  efficiency of water splitting  from the length of catalyst development  as you can see from the slide a good  catalyst  material should have high activity  stability  and selectivity at the same time  my focus was on how to increase its  activity  and stability by controlling the  semiconducting properties  of these materials from our research  we have found that by surface  modifications  we are able to activate the catalyst  facilitate  charge separations 