e that energy  and pull a lot of hydrogen out is  oxidize the fuel so in traditional steam  methane reforming which is how  95 of the world's hydrogen is made  there's a large surface reaction vessel  and a constant requirement to buy  natural gas  by contrast our process only  takes oxygen from the air and injects it  deep into the oil field  where reactions occur the net result of  which is hydrogen released  and then using a downhole filter the  only thing that can  go into the wellbore and be produced to  the surface  is pure hydrogen so in that way we save  the cost  of compressing carbon dioxide and  putting it in the ground  we save on the cost of the fuel because  there are our the world is full of  half full oil fields and we also save  on the cost of of the transportation of  the carbon molecule  so from a steam methane reforming versus  proton technologies perspective we have  no fuel cost  our operational cost and capital cost  are far far  lower and i think that hydrogen priced  meaningfully below  the price of natural gas is the way to  quickly  get society to adapt people  didn't get off go from whale oil to  kerosene because they loved whales  they did it because it was far far less  expensive and more convenient to use  kerosene instead of whale oil  similarly i believe this transition  comes down to price  if we want to go fast in the absence of  subsidies  we need to very uh find ways like  protons technology  that can price hydrogen below natural  gas  thanks very much  great thank you so much grant finally we  have  abraham gomez fadales abraham is a  research officer  at the national research council of  canada abraham the floor is yours  thanks for the introduction and thanks  all for your attendance  as phil mentioned my name is abraham  gomez vidalias  i'm a research officer at the national  research council of canada  and my current work is related to  hydrogen production development of  biosensor and bioelectrochemical  conversion of carbo